# What must NOT go into the .vsix.
#
# The hard constraint this file enforces, alongside having zero runtime
# dependencies: the packaged extension ships no third-party scanner code and no
# scanner assets. It invokes the `ash` the user installed. Nothing here is a
# size optimization -- `test/fixtures/` in particular contains a SARIF report and
# deliberately vulnerable-looking Python, neither of which belongs in a
# published artifact.
#
# `dependencies` in package.json is empty, so vsce bundles no node_modules at
# all; the entry below is belt-and-braces against that changing without anyone
# revisiting this file.

.vscode/**
.vscode-test/**
node_modules/**

# Sources. Only the compiled `out/src/**` ships.
src/**
test/**
**/*.ts
**/*.map

# Toolchain and local build state.
#
# assert-vsix-contents.py is the gate that checks this artifact, so it is build
# tooling and must not be inside the thing it checks. It was, on the first build
# after it was written -- vsce includes everything not listed here, and a new file
# at the package root ships by default. The gate caught itself: `extension/
# assert-vsix-contents.py` came back as an unexpected-member violation, which is
# the exact-member rule earning its place on a real mistake rather than a
# hypothetical one.
*.py
# Bytecode from running the gate. Python writes it beside the source on import,
# so the drift check (`--assert-tables-match-gate`, which imports the wheel gate)
# mints a __pycache__ here as a side effect. It shipped once: `*.py` above does
# not match `.pyc`, and a compiled object in a published artifact is exactly what
# this gate exists to refuse. Caught by three detectors at once -- unexpected
# member, vendor-directory on `__pycache__`, and native-binary on `.pyc`.
__pycache__/**
*.pyc
tsconfig.json
eslint.config.mjs
.gitignore
.npmrc
out/test/**
out/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo
coverage/**
*.vsix
